Task 1: Using Ctrl+R for Command History Search
Subtask 1.1: Basic Reverse Search
Open your terminal

Type and execute a few random commands to populate history:

echo "Hello World"
ls -l
date
whoami
Press Ctrl+R to initiate reverse search

Start typing "echo" - you should see the most recent "echo" command appear

Press Enter to execute the found command or press Ctrl+C to cancel

Expected Outcome:
The most recent matching command appears and can be executed.

Troubleshooting:
If nothing appears, ensure:

You've actually typed commands first (history must have entries)
Your terminal supports this key combination
Try stty -a to verify key bindings
Subtask 1.2: Navigating Search Results
Press Ctrl+R again while in search mode to cycle through older matches
Use Ctrl+S to search forward (may require terminal configuration)
Press Ctrl+G to exit search without executing anything
Key Concept:
Bash search is "reverse" because it starts from the most recent command and goes backward.

Task 2: Advanced History Search Techniques
Subtask 2.1: Searching for Specific Patterns
Execute these commands to create diverse history entries:

grep "error" /var/log/syslog
find ~ -name "*.txt"
docker ps -a
Search for commands containing "docker":

Press Ctrl+R
Type "docker"
Verify the correct command appears
Search for commands with both "find" and "name":

Press Ctrl+R
Type "find name"
Expected Outcome:
You can find commands using partial patterns and multiple keywords.

Subtask 2.2: Modifying Found Commands
Find a command using Ctrl+R
Before executing:
Use left/right arrows to move cursor
Edit the command as needed
Press Enter to execute modified command
Example:
Find ls -l, then modify to ls -la before executing.

Task 3: Creating Custom History Search Aliases
Subtask 3.1: Permanent History Configuration
Open your Bash configuration file:

nano ~/.bashrc
Add these configuration options for enhanced history:

# Increase history size
HISTSIZE=5000
HISTFILESIZE=10000

# Append to history instead of overwriting
shopt -s histappend

# Ignore duplicate commands and space-prefixed commands
HISTCONTROL=ignoreboth
Save and apply changes:

source ~/.bashrc
Subtask 3.2: Creating Search Aliases
Add these useful aliases to your ~/.bashrc:

# Quick history search aliases
alias hs='history | grep'
alias hsi='history | grep -i'
Apply changes:

source ~/.bashrc
Test the aliases:

hs docker
hsi ERROR
Expected Outcome:
You can now search history with simple alias commands, with case-sensitive and insensitive options.

Subtask 3.3: Advanced History Management
Add this function to ~/.bashrc for timestamped history:

function hst {
  history | awk '{print $2 " " $3 "\t" $4 "\t" $5FS$6FS$7FS$8FS$9FS$10}' | sort | uniq -c | sort -nr | head -n 20
}
Apply and test:

source ~/.bashrc
hst
Key Concept:
This shows your 20 most frequently used commands with usage counts.
